diagnosis_text:
A full diagnostic sweep of all devices in the path from pc12 through its gateway r12 (GG segment, 120.1.1.1) and upstream router r11 revealed no fault. The topology-wide reachability probe shows 0% packet loss in both directions between pc12 and pc0. The GG segment (pc12.eth0 ↔ r12.eth1) is UP on both ends with zero errors, zero drops, and zero carrier-change events beyond the normal Kathara startup interface-rename sequence visible in dmesg. No flap script or background process was found on pc12, r12, or any other device. Traffic-control inspection (tc_snapshot) confirmed no qdisc shaping on any interface. infra_sweep found no ACL rules, no addressing anomalies, and no ARP poisoning. r12 uses EIGRP rather than OSPF and has a stable default route via r11 (112.1.1.1); r11 in turn holds a valid EIGRP route to 120.0.0.0/8 via r12, confirming the full bidirectional forwarding path. pressure_sweep found no stressed or overloaded devices. The OSPF anomalies noted in the snapshot (r12 has no OSPF network statements, r13 has ospfd down, r8 has a local area 0.0.0.2 not shared by any peer) are real OSPF misconfigurations but none lie in the pc12 data path and none produce any current packet loss. The user's report of intermittent drops is unconfirmed: without a flap mechanism, carrier-change history, or any routing or service instability, the claim cannot be attributed to any injected fault in this lab. Root cause: no fault present — the reported intermittent outage has no supporting mechanism or historical trace in the current lab state. Fix: no corrective action required; monitor for recurrence under live traffic conditions and re-examine if carrier-change counters accumulate or a flap process is later observed.
=== END ===